<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Maramureș</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Maramureș is a historical region in the north-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carpathian-mountains">Carpathians</a>, on the upper <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tisza">Tisza</a>, today divided between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romania">Romania</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ukraine">Ukraine</a>. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a> it was a Hungarian county in which Romanian nobles kept local autonomy; two of them, Dragoș and Bogdan, crossed the mountains in the 14th century to found Moldavia. It is known for its wooden churches, several of which are a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/maramures.jpg" alt="Maramureș" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Chainwit. · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:2023-APR_-_Memorialul_Sighet_(Exterior)_IMG_09.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
